[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one bug, one fix queued. It was not one bug. Its
reports were merged on the subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and a subject line says only that a date is wrong. Read by tenant, form and what the body shows,
the ticket holds three problems. Each has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 closed on 5 October with the parser fix. That settled two of its three problems. The
one-day moves have no fix and are still open: ticket #3.
</Callout>

## Where each problem stands

Engineering update on #4471, closed 5 October:

- `parser`: **fixed** by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Ticket #1 closed.
- `hint`: **won't fix**, the `booking-v3` form is retired. Ticket #2 closed as not planned.
- `one-day`: **open**. The update does not mention it, and the parser fix cannot fix it: these
  dates move by a day, they do not swap. Ticket #3 stays open.

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

Counts are per report id: the export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice.
They are counted the desk's way (`causes.ts`): every report under the cause it is filed by. The
`one-day` reports stay counted under `parser`, where they are filed, and get their own ticket.
The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and does not match the cause; the
tenant's locale and booking form decide it (`causes.ts`, `locales.ts`).

## The three problems

### `parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first

Every tenant in #4471 writes dates day first. The shared parser reads them month first, so day
and month swap: 7 April becomes 4 July (R-41200). It hits every tenant, through the app, email
and the `booking-v2` form. Fixed on 5 October.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type month first

The `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date the
tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint says, so the date swaps before the parser
sees it (R-41241, R-41305, R-41387). Only reports through the form of a `booking-v3` tenant
(Alder Physio, Copper Studio, Pale Harbour). The parser fix did not touch it; retiring
`booking-v3` ends it.

### `one-day`: the date moves by one day, not by a swap

These reports show no swap. The date lands one day early or late: 12 May shows as 11 May
(R-41202), only June bookings are off by a day (R-41209), the invite comes before the booking
(R-41236), Lisbon gets Tuesday and Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting (R-41344), Tuesday 6
October shows as Monday (R-41620). That points at time zones or summer time, not at date order.
The desk read them this way (`desk-notes.json`) and files them under `parser` by tenant, so the
parser fix was taken to close them. It did not.

## Lessons

- A subject line tells you a date is wrong, not why. Split by cause before you count.
- Count report ids, not rows.
- An intake tag is a guess. Check it against the tenant before you trust it.
- A ticket closes when its fix ships. Check that the fix covers every problem merged into it.

